WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.129
Wardrobe that was too outlandish for Prince,
communicating in a secret language, and a

00:00:05.129 --> 00:00:07.689
cameo from a "90210" icon.

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:11.130
There's a lot to process with Luc Besson's
cult classic, "The Fifth Element," so forgive

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:14.320
yourself for not noticing a few things on
your first viewing.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.109
"The Fifth Element" has a truly star-studded
cast.

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:22.350
It's not every day you see people like Bruce
Willis, Milla Jovovich, Ian Holm, and Gary

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.300
Oldman all sharing the screen.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.019
Even the movie's smaller characters are played
by excellent actors like Brion James and Tom

00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:32.480
Lister Jr. — and then of course there's
Chris Tucker's character, Ruby Rhod.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.210
The first time you watch the movie, it's easy
to be so starstruck that you actually miss

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.590
one or two big stars who get featured.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.280
On a second pass, though, you'll notice that
even extremely minor characters are played

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.460
by recognizable actors.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.770
There's a reason, for instance, why Billy
looks so familiar in the beginning of the

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:48.770
movie.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:53.590
Billy is a research assistant who witnesses
aliens landing on Earth in 1914.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:57.020
He's got a very small part to play in the
movie, which is a little strange considering

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.230
he's played by Luke Perry.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:04.930
By 1997, Perry was an established star, with
seven years of "Beverly Hills 90210" and plenty

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.930
of other roles under his belt.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:11.320
Perry is the kind of actor who regularly showed
up in unexpected places, but even when he

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.400
doesn't have that much to do in a movie, he
brings a certain level of charm to a character.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.409
At its heart, "The Fifth Element" is an adventure
story.

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:22.780
Korben's chance encounter with Leeloo sends
his life spiraling in a new direction, and

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:27.340
the two of them end up racing across deep
space to save the universe from evil.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:31.450
The movie introduces some dazzling locations
and sci-fi concepts, but it doesn't spend

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.240
nearly as much time developing them as it
does rushing through the plot.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.130
That's why every time you rewatch the movie,
you'll notice new small details about the

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:41.850
world — and sometimes they're a little concerning.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:46.910
By the end of the movie, we don't know all
that much about New York City circa 2263,

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:51.469
but we've gotten enough details to learn that
the city is a borderline dystopia.

00:01:51.469 --> 00:01:55.060
Korben's life as a taxi driver gives us a
glimpse into the capitalist nightmare that

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:58.969
seems to have taken over this version of the
future, and his chase with the police lets

00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:03.180
us know that New York really needs to consider
some drastic reform measures.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:08.170
As Korben's taxi races past a police hover
car early in the movie, we see that the NYPD

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:14.319
of 2263 wears full metal body armor at all
times, even if they're at a fast food drive-thru.

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:18.290
When the police start to pursue Korben, it
quickly becomes clear that collateral damage

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:19.700
isn't a concern for them.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:25.120
They all but tear apart the city trying to
get Korben, and they don't even succeed.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.569
When Leeloo lands in the back of Korben's
taxi, she doesn't know where she is, and she

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:31.750
definitely doesn't know how to speak English.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.480
"Boom."

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:40.050
"Boom, yeah, I understand boom."

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:43.890
Considering that she's just been essentially
resurrected and brought into the future from

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:47.879
thousands of years in the past, she learns
how to communicate remarkably quickly.

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:51.870
Though she picks up more and more English
as the movie progresses, most of the time

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.330
Leeloo speaks a language beyond anyone's comprehension.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:59.670
In reality, the words that Leeloo speaks were
created by director Luc Besson just for this

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:00.670
movie.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:04.840
Besson spent 15 years developing the world
of "The Fifth Element," and in all that time

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.459
he also spent some effort creating a dictionary
for the Divine Language.

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:13.310
Besson invented around 400 words so that Leeloo
could, in theory, say everything she needs

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:16.140
to say through the entire movie in a real
language.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.660
Besson and Milla Jovovich both became so fluent
in the Divine Language that they even used

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:23.010
it to communicate with each other while filming.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:26.940
"The Fifth Element" gets more mileage than
most movies out of playing with tropes and

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.460
character archetypes.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:32.439
The story has a traditional big evil and a
chosen one destined to defeat it.

00:03:32.439 --> 00:03:36.900
Leeloo and Korben both get to play out different
takes on the hero archetype, while Gary Oldman's

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.629
Zorg stands in for the movie's real villain.

00:03:39.629 --> 00:03:43.340
The movie sets up a story that's initially
familiar, but it subverts the usual formula

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.930
whenever it can.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:48.379
One of the oddest parts about "The Fifth Element"
is that the heroes and the villains never

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:51.390
actually see each other face to face.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.659
Obviously the big space evil is a kind of
amorphous entity that would probably have

00:03:54.659 --> 00:03:58.750
trouble standing on the surface of Earth,
but Korben never even meets Zorg, the evil's

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:02.650
human representative, while Leeloo only runs
into him briefly.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:05.790
But that doesn't mean they have no connection
to him whatsoever.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:09.700
The first time they watch the movie, most
people probably miss a small detail on Korben's

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.280
termination notice after he loses his job.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.961
Zorg owns the company that fires Korben, so
even though the two never directly meet, their

00:04:16.961 --> 00:04:19.729
conflict is still personal.

00:04:19.729 --> 00:04:23.970
According to "The Fifth Element," by 2263
humanity will have spread out to the stars

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.790
and made contact with alien civilizations.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:30.350
The future looks and sounds amazing, but when
you really think about the people who are

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:33.590
in charge of the planet, you might have second
thoughts about that.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:37.180
"My philosophy Mr. President is shoot first
and ask questions later.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.460
I don't like uninvited guests."

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.289
Earth's military has a big role to play in
"The Fifth Element."

00:04:42.289 --> 00:04:45.940
They're the ones who make first contact with
the great evil that employs Zorg, and they

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:49.910
eventually help send Korben and Leeloo on
their journey to save the world.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:54.040
It might be fair to say all's well that ends
well, but the movie shows us over and over

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:57.850
again how incompetent the future leadership
of Earth really is.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:02.259
The President, played by Tom "Tiny" Lister
Jr., never seems to have any idea what's really

00:05:02.259 --> 00:05:03.540
going on.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:07.509
He's willing to take advice from Ian Holm's
Cornelius, despite having very little evidence

00:05:07.509 --> 00:05:11.590
that the priest knows what he's talking about
when it comes to epic space evils.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:16.509
The military, on the other hand, is represented
by Brion James' character General Munro.

00:05:16.509 --> 00:05:21.160
You'd want a general to have a sound tactical
mind, but Korben manages to outsmart Munro,

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:25.220
leaving him trapped in a freezer in his apartment
while he escapes with Leeloo.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.970
The movie is definitely saying something about
people in power, but it's also depicting a

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.350
future we'd all be better off avoiding.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:36.400
"The Fifth Element" is filled with great performances,
but Chris Tucker's appearance as Ruby Rhod

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.680
is one of the most memorable parts of the
entire movie.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.380
From clothing to hair, Ruby's sense of style
is something that you won't forget after seeing

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:44.380
it.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.900
Tucker brings a tremendous amount of energy
to the role, helping Ruby steal scenes so

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:52.060
thoroughly that even the characters in the
movie seem to be a bit stunned by Tucker's

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:53.060
performance.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:56.531
It turns out that everyone on set probably
was just as surprised by Tucker as anyone

00:05:56.531 --> 00:05:58.630
who's ever watched "The Fifth Element."

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:02.820
Tucker told EW that when he took the part,
hardly anyone knew what the role was supposed

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.940
to be, including himself.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:08.190
All he knew was that Prince was supposed to
play the role, but he passed it up because

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.690
the costumes were too outlandish.

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:13.940
Tucker didn't know what to think, until he
saw the costume for himself.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:14.940
He said -

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.660
"I was kind of like 'Whoof, this is a deep,
deep character.'

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:21.250
But all that stuff really helped me get into
character."

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.860
Gary Oldman is one of the greatest character
actors of his generation.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.650
He's played some heroic characters, but Oldman
really shines as a villain.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:32.600
Whether he's playing a shapeshifting vampire
in "Bram Stoker's Dracula" or the crazed mayor

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.930
of a post-apocalyptic town in "The Book of
Eli," Oldman brings a chilling energy to all

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:38.370
his baddies.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:42.669
Amidst all the aliens and shiny scenery, it
can be easy to lose track of Oldman's character

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:46.560
Zorg in "The Fifth Element," but the second
time through you'll realize how terrifying

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:48.300
he really is.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:52.520
What makes Zorg so scary isn't that he's working
for a terrible evil, but that communicating

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.419
with his evil boss seems to shake Zorg to
his very core.

00:06:56.419 --> 00:07:00.440
On the phone with the evil entity, Zorg's
head spontaneously starts bleeding, and his

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:03.069
own terror is very apparent.

00:07:03.069 --> 00:07:07.050
Despite his fear, he keeps trying to help
the evil grow in power.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:11.099
Oldman's performance in the movie is even
more impressive if you know the context surrounding

00:07:11.099 --> 00:07:12.099
it.

00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:15.970
In an interview with Role Recall, the actor
admitted that he only took the role as a favor

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:16.970
to Luc Besson.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:21.009
He didn't even read the script before agreeing
to be in the movie, and it doesn't sound like

00:07:21.009 --> 00:07:22.979
he's a huge fan of the finished product.

00:07:22.979 --> 00:07:27.129
When told that "The Fifth Element" has become
a cult classic, Oldman just laughed and said

00:07:27.129 --> 00:07:28.129
-

00:07:28.129 --> 00:07:29.910
"That's the wacky world we live in."

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:34.210
As a character, Leeloo is obviously based
on "chosen one" tropes that are as old as

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:35.669
time itself.

00:07:35.669 --> 00:07:39.300
The movie repeatedly says that she's supposed
to be a perfect being.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:43.190
It's not just that Leeloo is destined to save
the world, but also that she has some borderline

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:44.990
magical abilities.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:49.050
She's an incredibly fast learner, and all
but a champion fighter with little to no training

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:50.599
whatsoever.

00:07:50.599 --> 00:07:54.110
Korben plays a central role in the story,
but it's really Leeloo who's the hero of "The

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:55.110
Fifth Element."

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:59.400
As much as she's based on mythological heroes
from stories that came before her, Leeloo

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.660
has also gone on to inspire other sci-fi heroes.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:07.050
Her influence can easily be seen in characters
like River from "Firefly" and "Serenity,"

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:11.690
but she has a very particular similarity with
another major '90s sci-fi "chosen one."

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:15.870
In the movie Leeloo learns how to fight by
watching video feeds on a small screen.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.620
She basically downloads the knowledge into
her head.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:22.949
Two years after the debut of "The Fifth Element,"
Neo learns his own fighting techniques using

00:08:22.949 --> 00:08:25.050
a very similar method in "The Matrix."

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:31.889
"I know kung fu."

00:08:31.889 --> 00:08:35.289
There are plenty of '90s sci-fi movies that
don't hold up visually today.

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:39.370
CGI has come a long way in the past three
decades, and movies that went all in on the

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:42.169
technology back then are really starting to
show their age.

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:46.470
"The Fifth Element" has no shortage of special
effects, but the movie still looks fantastic

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:50.630
today because so much of what happens on screen
was created practically.

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:54.500
Luc Besson knew that his movie was going to
demand an excessive amount of visual effects

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:59.720
— it took more than 160 people to create
the 220 shots that made it into the movie,

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:03.260
and the team filmed plenty more that didn't
survive the editing room.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:08.029
Besson employed model makers and artists to
make his world as realistic as possible.

00:09:08.029 --> 00:09:10.680
The spaceships shown in the movie are real
models.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:14.880
So is Korben's taxi, and even some of the
New York City streets that he flies it down.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:18.670
A second viewing gives one plenty of time
to really appreciate the level of effort that

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:22.500
went into making "The Fifth Element" as visually
engaging as it is.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.329
Unfortunately, not everything that you notice
about "The Fifth Element" when watching it

00:09:26.329 --> 00:09:27.839
again is good.

00:09:27.839 --> 00:09:31.240
There are plenty of things to praise about
the movie, from the performances to the music

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:35.450
to the visual effects, but decades later it
also stands out for being filled to the brim

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:37.790
with misogynist stereotypes.

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:41.560
There's no way around it: "The Fifth Element"
does a terrible job depicting women.

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:45.770
Leeloo is basically the only human woman on
screen the entire movie, and even though she's

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:50.160
a hardcore action hero, she still ends up
falling into the damsel in distress role more

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:51.160
than once.

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:55.420
As badass as Leeloo may be on her own, the
movie doesn't let her be a hero without the

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:56.829
help of Korben.

00:09:56.829 --> 00:10:00.340
"But she's more fragile than she seems.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:05.440
She needs your help… and your love."

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:09.410
She's also wearing little more than some strategically
placed bandages for the first third of the

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:10.470
film.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:14.279
It's the other women in the movie that get
a really problematic portrayal.

00:10:14.279 --> 00:10:18.491
Korben's mother and ex-wife, heard only as
voices talking to Korben over the phone, are

00:10:18.491 --> 00:10:22.550
depicted as greedy nags who won't let Korben
live his life in peace.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:26.310
The movie plays these moments for laughs as
Korben rolls his eyes and desperately tries

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.280
to avoid interacting with them as much as
possible.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:33.190
Those cringe-worthy moments don't ruin the
film by any means, but they may tarnish your

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:34.959
memories of it on that second viewing.

